th\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a31417d1/img_007_818a3e09.png\" alt=\"I equals 600\"></span>. This number declines by 15% per year, so <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a31417d1/img_008_62712769.png\" alt=\"r equals 15\"></span>. Substituting these values into the equation produces <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a31417d1/img_009_9b83e595.png\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 1 minus the fraction 15 over 100, close parenthesis, to the t power  \"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a31417d1/img_010_49809899.png\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 0 point 8 5, close parenthesis, to the t power\"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from errors made when representing the percent decline. Choices C and D are incorrect. These equations model exponential increases in CD sales, not exponential decreases.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":11,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959623,"pPcc":"SAT#P","questionId":"a31417d1","skill_cd":"P.C.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Nonlinear functions","createDate":1691007959623,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Advanced Math","ibn":"028933-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"P","uId":"52b5ca3b-d4b1-47d1-b3d4-67b848dfb1c7","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"028933-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">From 2005 through 2014, the number of music CDs sold in the United States declined each year by approximately 15% of the number sold the preceding year. In 2005, approximately 600 million CDs were sold in the United States. Of the following, which best models <span class=\"italic\">C</span>, the number of millions of CDs sold in the United States, <span class=\"italic\">t</span> years after 2005?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 0 point 1 5, close parenthesis, raised to the t power\"></span></span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 0 point 8 5, close parenthesis, raised to the t power\"></span></span></p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 1 point 1 5, close parenthesis, raised to the t power\"></span></span></p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 1 point 8 5, close parenthesis, raised to the t power\"></span></span></p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"b","rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. A model for a quantity <span class=\"italic\">C</span> that decreases by a certain percentage per time period <span class=\"italic\">t</span> is an exponential equation in the form <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"C equals, I times, open parenthesis, 1 minus, the fraction r over 100, close parenthesis, to the t power\"></span>, where <span class=\"italic\">I</span> is the initial value at time <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"t equals 0\"></span> for <span class=\"italic\">r</span>% annual decline. It&rsquo;s given that <span class=\"italic\">C</span> is the number of millions of CDs sold in the United States and that <span class=\"italic\">t</span> is the number of years after 2005. It&rsquo;s also given that 600 million CDs were sold at time <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"t equals 0\"></span>, so <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"I equals 600\"></span>. This number declines by 15% per year, so <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r equals 15\"></span>. Substituting these values into the equation produces <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 1 minus the fraction 15 over 100, close parenthesis, to the t power  \"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"C equals, 600 times, open parenthesis, 0 point 8 5, close parenthesis, to the t power\"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from errors made when representing the percent decline. Choices C and D are incorrect. These equations model exponential increases in CD sales, not exponential decreases.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959623,"updateDate":1691007959623}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959623, 1691007959623),
    ($SATQ$a391ed22$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$070623-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Equivalent expressions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="stimulus_reference " xmlns="http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1"><p class="standalone_statement style:1 "><span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_000_2ca6d8f2.png" alt="open parenthesis, one half x, plus three halves, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, three halves x, plus one half, close parenthesis"></span></span></p></div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">The expression above is equivalent to <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_001_2c7da1b7.png" alt="a, x squared, plus b x, plus c "></span></span>, where <span class="italic">a</span>, <span class="italic">b</span>, and <span class="italic">c</span> are constants. What is the value of <span class="italic">b</span>?</p>
$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["5/2","2.5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_002_35c68a84.png" alt="five halves"></span>. The expression <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_003_2ca6d8f2.png" alt="open parenthesis, one half x, plus three halves, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, three halves x, plus one half, close parenthesis"></span> can be written in the form <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_004_2c7da1b7.png" alt="a, x squared, plus b x, plus c"></span>, where <span class="italic">a</span>, <span class="italic">b</span>, and <span class="italic">c</span> are constants, by multiplying out the expression using the distributive property of multiplication over addition. The result is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_005_20b4a426.png" alt="one half x, times three halves x, plus, one half x, times one half, plus, three halves times three halves x, plus, three halves times one half"></span>. This expression can be rewritten by multiplying as indicated to give <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_006_8901d33b.png" alt="three fourths x squared, plus, one fourth x, plus, nine fourths x, plus, three fourths"></span>, which can be simplified to <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_007_183b93ac.png" alt="three fourths x squared, plus, ten fourths x, plus, three fourths"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_008_f7517a79.png" alt="three fourths x squared, plus, five halves x, plus, three fourths"></span>. This is in the form <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_009_2c7da1b7.png" alt="a, x squared, plus b x, plus c"></span>, where the value of <span class="italic">b</span> is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/a391ed22/img_010_35c68a84.png" alt="five halves"></span>. Note that 5/2 and 2.5 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>
